  Shared Model-Exchange algebraic refresh for the generated C components.

  One kernel, included by every C component target and by a device unit,
  settles algebraic coordinates exactly as the linked ME kernel does: the
  construction-issued derivative and algebraic refresh plans run stage by
  stage, and every
  projection stage solves its block with the linked projection algorithms of
  `rumoca-solver` `runtime::projection` (singleton isolation, the affine
  solve with its reduced elimination, the torn reduced Newton, and the dense
  block Newton), under the one numerical policy of
  `rumoca_eval_solve::projection_policy`. Each C function names the Rust
  function it mirrors.

  Two kinds of unit include it. A C component target defines `ModelInstance`
  (with `time`, `y`, `p`), `Y_LEN`, `RMC_LOG(m, message)` and
  `RMC_LOG_AT(m, category, message)` itself and calls the kernel macros with
  `device` false. A device unit defines none of them: `device_instance`
  defines `ModelInstance` and `Y_LEN` and no log, and `device_kernel` emits
  the kernel (see the comment above `device_instance` for what such a unit
  provides). A lane build (`RMC_LANE_STATUS`, which every device build is)
  logs no fallback or fault report: it keeps its reports in the instance's
  `rmc_lane_status` for `rmc_lane_report` (SPEC_0044 ME-PROJ-003).
